  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Sunday)\nIssued at 1229 PM CDT MON AUG 31 2026\n\nThe region remains under the influence of a large upper ridge. To\nour south, an area of low pressure over the Northern Gulf will\ncontinue to track westward over the next couple of days. The NHC\nhas issued an advisory for a Potential Tropical Cyclone, which\nmay strengthen into a tropical storm tonight as it nears the\nsouthwest Louisiana/Upper Texas coast. As we head through the\nwork week, the upper ridge will expand eastward, steering the\ntropical system westward across the Gulf.\n\nDrier air and increased subsidence will maintain mainly rain-free\nconditions across the central third of the area. We anticipate\nlow chances for diurnally driven convection across our southern\nareas where moisture remains elevated. Across the far north,\nspeckled showers may develop within an outer convergence zone.\nWhile severe weather is not expected due to minimal wind shear,\nsufficient instability exists to support an isolated stronger\nstorm during peak heating.\n\nA warming trend begins this week, with temperatures climbing into\nthe mid-to-upper 90s by midweek. Humid conditions will push heat\nindices to 105-108\u00b0 each afternoon, likely necessitating daily \nHeat Advisories. Probabilistic heat risk data indicates a 50-60% \nchance of major or higher heat risk heading into Labor Day \nWeekend. We will increase heat safety messaging throughout the \nweek; emphasize the importance of hydration and limiting outdoor \nexposure.\n\n16",
